<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maria Beatrice d'Este, Duchess of Massa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1750">1750</a>–<a href="/years/1829">1829</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-este">House of Este</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maria Beatrice d'Este (1750–1829) was heiress of the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massa-tuscany">Massa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carrara">Carrara</a> through her mother, Maria Teresa Cybo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaspina">Malaspina</a>, and of the Este dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a> through her father. In 1771 she married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archduke-ferdinand-karl-of-austria">Archduke Ferdinand Karl of Austria</a>, founding the House of Austria-Este. Restored to Massa in 1814 after Napoleonic rule, she left it on her death to her son Francis IV.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maria-beatrice-d-este-duchess-of-massa.jpg" alt="Maria Beatrice d'Este, Duchess of Massa" width="320" height="458" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Workshop of Anton von Maron · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marie-B%C3%A9atrice_d%27Este.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Maria Beatrice d'Este was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a> on 7 April 1750, the eldest child of Ercole Rinaldo d'Este, heir to the Duchy of Modena and Reggio, and Maria Teresa Cybo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaspina">Malaspina</a>, Duchess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massa-tuscany">Massa</a> and Princess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carrara">Carrara</a>. Her parents' marriage was unhappy and they lived apart. Her only brother died aged four months in 1753, leaving her the only surviving child. Modena and Reggio followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salic-law">Salic law</a>, which barred female succession, whereas Massa and Carrara did not. To keep the duchy from being absorbed by the Empire, her grandfather Francis III concluded two treaties with the House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in 1753, one public and one secret, engaging her to Archduke Leopold, a son of Empress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1761 Leopold became heir to Tuscany, and in 1763, despite her father's opposition, the families substituted Archduke Ferdinand Karl, four years younger than his betrothed. The Diet of Regensburg ratified his future investiture in January 1771, and the couple married in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> in October, founding the House of Austria-Este. Mozart's Ascanio in Alba and Hasse's Il Ruggiero were performed at the festivities. They lived in Milan for nearly 25 years and had ten children.</p><p>When her mother died in 1790 Maria Beatrice became Duchess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massa-tuscany">Massa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carrara">Carrara</a>. She proved a scrupulous administrator but never moved to the duchy. After the French conquest of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> she lived mostly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, at the court of her nephew-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii">Francis II</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>. Her youngest daughter married him in 1808, and she was mother-in-law also of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-emmanuel-i-of-sardinia">Victor Emmanuel I of Sardinia</a> and of Elector Charles Theodore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Under the principle of legitimacy advocated by Metternich at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a>, she was restored as sovereign of the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massa-tuscany">Massa</a> and Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carrara">Carrara</a>, and the imperial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lunigiana">Lunigiana</a> were also granted to her. By an agreement in December she ceded the Lunigiana fiefs to her son Francis IV, who had been installed as Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a> and Reggio.</p><p>When she died in 1829, Francis IV succeeded her in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massa-tuscany">Massa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carrara">Carrara</a> and in a few years absorbed her domains into the Este States. The House of Austria-Este ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a> until 1859.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Maria Beatrice d'Este, Duchess of Massa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The heiress <b>Maria Beatrice Cybo d'Este</b> married Ferdinand of Austria-Este.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-massa-and-carrara">Duchy of Massa and Carrara</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.65,10.93&amp;year=1801" aria-label="Modena on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Maria Beatrice d'Este, Duchess of Massa"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5661 3729h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5661 3729h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.65,10.93&amp;year=1801">What was here in 1801? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
